      "id": "review::.::holistic::high_level_elegance::interface_contract_mismatch",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 2,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "summary": "interfaces.py ABCs define narrow contracts that don't match implementation surface",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "high_level_elegance",
        "related_files": [
          "src/analyzer/interfaces.py",
          "src/analyzer/datasources.py"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "TransactionSource ABC defines only get_transactions(year), but HouseTransactionSource exposes fetch_metadata, fetch_and_cache_pdfs, parse_cached_pdfs",
          "PriceSource ABC defines only get_prices(tickers, start, end), but YFinancePriceSource also uses Database for caching internally",
          "The interfaces serve as loose documentation rather than real architectural contracts"
        ],
        "suggestion": "Either widen the ABCs to reflect the actual public API of their implementors (including lifecycle methods like fetch/parse), or acknowledge them as marker interfaces and document that these are partial contracts. At this codebase size the current approach works but will mislead new engineers about what these classes actually do.",
